#{[17592186119631 :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,\\mathcal{B}.$ Since we are told that $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & =\\underbrace{(-1)}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}3\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119632 :question/worked-solution "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n$$\nM =\t \\left[{\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & - 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & - 11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & - 8\\end{array}}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix above corresponds to the following system of equations: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ -4{x}_{2} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2 =-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 + 3 \\left( -1 \\right)  = -5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx_1 = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$"] [17592186119633 :question/worked-solution "We need to find $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{3}} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above system as an augmented matrix $M$ and reducing using Gaussian elimination, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=7 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} &=\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 =2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-9x_2 - 2 = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2 = -1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_2=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+3(-1) = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_1 = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$"] [17592186120352 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186120352 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 6\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-2)+2=0.$"] [17592186120357 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\}$, where\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}$?"] [17592186120358 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186120358 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ 0 &=4,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere the fourth equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186120379 :question/problem "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, if $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ and \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186120380 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$, where\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1} + x_{2}$?"] [17592186120382 :question/problem "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, if $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$, and\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186120525 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$"] [17592186120526 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\}$, where\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1}$?"] [17592186162586 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & {\\color{red}{-1}} \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{red}{1}} & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""] [17592186235392 :question/problem "$$\nX+Y=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Knowing that the matrix representation $T=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}_{11} & {t}_{12} \\\\ {t}_{21} & {t}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=XZ+YZ,\n$$\n\nfind the values ${t}_{ij}.$\n\n${t}_{11}=$ {{field-1}}\n\n${t}_{12}=$ {{field-2}}\n\n${t}_{21}=$ {{field-3}}\n\n${t}_{22}=$ {{field-4}}"] [17592186247247 :question/problem "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, if $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ and \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186247247 :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathcal{B}.$ Since we are told that $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & =\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}6\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}-7\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -30 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247248 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$"] [17592186247253 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & \\leftrightarrow {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & \\leftrightarrow {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & \\leftrightarrow {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."] [17592186247261 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -28 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -14 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."] [17592186275133 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & {\\color{red}{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{red}{-1}} & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {\\color{red}{-1}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\color{red}{1}} & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & {\\color{red}{1}} & 0 \\\\ {\\color{red}{0}} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the matrices are symmetric.\""] [17592186275134 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & {\\color{red}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{4}} & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""] [17592186348109 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\